{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788298118,"nonce":"Yzuv0iOOBb/NuvdLrhuiW00yZNA=","message":"## Follow-up: the `flee` verdict's citation was wrong, and is now fixed (`741e31c`)\n\nThe orchestrator caught a citation defect in the shipped `why` text. It is real, I have fixed it in all six places, and I am recording it rather than absorbing it quietly.\n\n**What was wrong.** The `flee` verdict's `why` said it \"fires at \u003c= 8 cells with line of sight (`FleeUtility.ShouldFleeFrom`, checkDistance:true)\". Both halves belong to the OTHER branch of the same method. `maxDist = 8f` (melee) / `Mathf.Clamp(CurrentEffectiveVerb.EffectiveRange * 0.66f, 2f, 20f)` (ranged) and the `NeedLOSToAll` scan flag are in `TryGetAttackNearbyEnemyJob` — and they are cited correctly in `attack-then-seek` and `attack-nearby`. They got copied onto the flee verdict, where they do not apply.\n\n**What `TryGetFleeJob` actually does.** Two halves, two different calls, and separating them says more than the wrong text did:\n\n- **TRIGGER** — it opens with `if (!SelfDefenseUtility.ShouldStartFleeing(pawn)) return null;`, and that gate is the *only* place distance and sight are tested: `ShouldFleeFrom(checkDistance: true, checkLOS: false)` over `ThingRequestGroup.AlwaysFlee`, and `(checkDistance: true, checkLOS: true)` over `ThingRequestGroup.AttackTarget` inside a `RegionTraverser.BreadthFirstTraverse` capped at 9 regions. `checkDistance:true` is `t.Position.InHorDistOf(pawn.Position, 8f)`.\n- **DESTINATION** — once triggered, `TryGetFleeJob` re-gathers the threat set at **all three** of its own call sites with `checkDistance: false, checkLOS: false` and passes the lot to `CellFinderLoose.GetFleeDest`. So the pawn flees from *every* hostile the caches hold, not the one that set it off.\n\n**That gap is the 150 cells.** One crow inside eight cells triggered it; the whole map scored where Captain ran to. The corrected `why` now carries that, which is strictly more useful than what it replaced.\n\n**The verdict logic is unchanged** — `flee` still wins, still decided above seek, and `on_contact`'s ordering is untouched. But `why` is the entire value of that field, and a field built to be trusted carrying a citation that names the wrong method is worse than one with no citation. That is the workspace `CLAUDE.md` essay's exact failure mode, on my own text.\n\n**Six sites, because a wrong citation written once was written five times** — as predicted. `SeekVerbs.cs` header consequences bullet, the `OnContact` resolution table, the `flee` verdict body, and `PostureSection`'s `flee_risk` comment; plus the `DESIGN.md` decisions entry, `playbook/seek-off-is-a-decision-to-flee.md`, and both `accept/b1b3060-posture.{py,md}` (where the phase-6 comment now cites `ShouldStartFleeing`'s `checkLOS:true` branch as the reason raiders must close before anything fires, instead of hand-waving at \"line of sight\"). The DESIGN entry **records the correction** rather than reading as if it were always right.\n\n**And the second half of the note.** `JobGiver_ConfigurableHostilityResponse.TryGiveJob` bails to null before it reads the mode at all — on `playerSettings == null || !UsesConfigurableHostilityResponse`, `PawnUtility.PlayerForcedJobNowOrSoon`, `pawn.Downed`, and an Anomaly `LordJob_PsychicRitual` lord. My `downed` and `player-controlled` verdicts leaned only on the seek side, which was half the reason. Both now cite the constant tree too: `downed` names the pre-switch `pawn.Downed` bail, and `player-controlled` names `PlayerForcedJobNowOrSoon` reading `CurJob.playerForced` (else the head of `jobs.jobQueue`) — a drafted order is `playerForced`. The four pre-switch bails are documented once above the resolution table so a future verdict does not have to rediscover them.\n\n**Verification.** `dotnet build -c Release` — `Build succeeded. 0 Warning(s) 0 Error(s)`. `git checkout -- Assemblies/` again; branch still carries no assembly, tree clean. Suite re-run: `--selftest` **26/26**, `--dry-run` **117 expectations**, both exit 0 read from `$?`. `spec/posture` is now `a83db72`, `d269b53`, `026b8e3`, `741e31c`.\n\nStill true and unchanged: **nothing on this branch has met a bench.**\n\nHousekeeping, acknowledged: my `comment.md` at the shared scratchpad root was almost certainly the file that clobbered another worker's — the other three had already namespaced theirs. Mine is now under `scratchpad/worker-c/` and everything I write there is namespaced from here on. Sorry for the time that cost.","files":null}]}